<section class="section" id="how-it-works">
  <h2>How the gate works</h2>
  <p>
    Point corral at a code change and the tests that are supposed to cover it.
    A decorrelated herd of models plants goal-violating faults in the code and
    runs <em>your</em> tests against each one in the selected execution substrate — measuring the kill
    rate by execution, never by a self-report. A different model reads the
    suite cold and critiques it; the writer attempts to author a test for
    each survivor. A proof is not guaranteed: only tests that pass on the
    unmodified code and fail on a survivor count. What comes out is a recorded
    verdict: <strong>certified</strong>, or <strong>needs-review</strong>. No
    one may be judge in their own cause.
  </p>
  <pre class="flow">change + its tests
  │
  ▼
[mutant-generator] plants goal-violating faults in the code
  │ each fault ↓
  ▼
run YOUR tests against each fault ──► kill-rate (never a self-report)
  │
  ├── faults your tests killed        → covered
  └── survivors (a fault slipped past) → [test-writer] attempts a killing test
                                          keep only execution-demonstrated proofs
  │
  ▼
[test-critic — a DIFFERENT model reads the suite cold]
  │
  ▼
RECORDED VERDICT ─ certified (kill-rate clears the bar, the writer neither
               │                failed nor wrote an unsound test)
               └─ needs-review (below the bar, a writer failure, or an exam too
                                small to judge — a human adjudicates)</pre>

  <div class="tech-grid">
    <div class="tech">
      <h3>Decorrelated by construction</h3>
      <p>
        Reusing one model in every role risks correlated blind spots.
        A cold run of the same model can still find something an earlier run missed;
        different model names or vendors do not guarantee independence.
        Corral holds one pair apart by rule: <code>CheckDecorrelation</code> refuses a run where the critic and the test-writer are the same model.
        The fault-planter and the writer may share a model, and a run whose
        seats all come from one vendor is allowed — the verdict says so ("every
        graded seat is …") rather than refusing it. Each role can be pointed
        at a different <em>vendor</em>, so a Gemini fault-planter with a Claude
        critic is one run. Mix cheap local models with frontier ones: bring
        anything OpenAI-compatible, or a local model, on your own key — no
        vendor lock-in.
      </p>
      <p>
        And decorrelate from whoever wrote the code. corral keeps the critic apart
        from the writer; it cannot see the <em>fourth</em> participant — the model
        that authored the file under audit. If that was Claude and you audit
        with Claude, the lineage that wrote the code is planting the faults and
        grading the tests for it. Point the roles at a different vendor than
        your code came from.
      </p>
      <a href="/docs/concepts/multi-model-herds/">Multi-model herds →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="tech">
      <h3>Certified by execution, not by a vibe</h3>
      <p>
        A suite's grade isn't an opinion. The kill rate is <strong>mutation
        testing, measured by execution on your machine or runner</strong>: your real command — <code>go test</code>, <code>pytest</code>, <code>npm test</code>, <code>cargo test</code>, anything — is run against each planted fault
        inside a sandbox by default (on the host with <code>--substrate workspace</code>), and the exit codes decide what died.
        A model never tells us it did well; a test that claims to catch a bug
        has to actually catch it, on a real run, on the record. Clear the bar
        with no blocking gap and the change is certified; fall short and it
        routes to a human as needs-review.
      </p>
      <a href="/docs/concepts/mutation-testing/">Certify by execution →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="tech">
      <h3>Survivors get a test-writing attempt, not a promise</h3>
      <p>
        A critic who only complains is furniture. When a planted fault slips
        past your suite, the <strong>test-writer</strong> attempts a test and
        corral checks it: runs the new test
        against the surviving fault and watches it fail, then against clean
        code and watches it pass. The gap comes back to you as <code>proven_missed</code> — a fault your suite missed and the exact test
        that catches it — not a lecture. It is a gap in your tests, not a bug
        found in your code; whether the test belongs is still your call.
        A writer that cannot prove a survivor leaves it unproven, not clean.
        Review the tests and run them in your full suite before merging:
        individual proofs do not guarantee that the tests work together.
      </p>
      <a href="/field-notes/your-tests-suck/">Three real verdicts →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="tech">
      <h3>A signed, tamper-evident verdict</h3>
      <p>
        A <code>certify --local</code> run ends in a record signed with your
        local certify key (<code>--out</code> writes it as a file anyone holding
        your public key can verify offline); a <code>--repo</code> run's ledger
        entry is signed when a certify key is configured and written unsigned
        when one is not (still hash-linked). Either way it carries the kill
        rate, the survivors, the written tests, and which model played which
        role — <code>ProducedBy: [mutant-generator, test-critic, test-writer]</code>,
        each named. A signature proves integrity under a public key you trust,
        not the truth of the claims or the independence of the signer.
        You control the local ledger: detecting removal from its tail requires
        an external record of the expected head. It is tamper-evident, not immutable.
      </p>
      <a href="/docs/concepts/trust-and-security/">Trust &amp; security →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="tech">
      <h3>Where the code runs</h3>
      <p>
        By default, the commands an audit runs — your tests against each
        mutant, the new test against the survivor, a review's reproduction
        scripts — run on a copy of your tree inside a sandbox (<code>bwrap</code> or a
        container on Linux, <code>sandbox-exec</code> on macOS): no network,
        your checkout untouched, a secret-free environment. One mode opts
        out: <code>--substrate workspace</code>, which the GitHub Action uses, runs
        them on the runner itself and mutates its checkout, because a
        disposable CI runner is the isolation boundary there. A coding-agent
        seat runs under that agent's own permissions. Either way the kill rate
        is a fact about what ran, not a number a model asserted.
      </p>
      <a href="/docs/concepts/trust-and-security/">The jail →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="tech">
      <h3>And it measures which model to trust</h3>
      <p>
        The record says which model actually <em>catches</em> bugs in which
        seat, proven by execution, not self-reported. <code>corral models
        rank</code> reads that back and ranks the seats — a number you can't
        flatter your way up. It never staffs one: you name every seat, so no
        audit picks its own auditors.
      </p>
      <a href="/docs/cli/corral/">The CLI reference →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

